<Docs slug="immich">
  <p>
    LightPhotos can upload finished photos straight to any Immich server you
    point it at — including one on a box in your closet. Open the export dialog
    and choose the <strong>Immich</strong> tab, then paste your server's address
    and an API key.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Immich is a photo library you host yourself.</strong> It is the
      closest thing to a self-hosted Google Photos, and LightPhotos treats it
      as a first-class export target.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your server address and API key are stored locally.</strong> They
      live in your browser's storage or the desktop build's config — never
      sent anywhere except your own Immich server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Let the server handle duplicates.</strong> Immich already
      deduplicates by content hash, so re-exporting the same photo is safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The web version shows the Immich tab with a note</strong> that
      browser security may block the connection if your server doesn't allow
      cross-origin requests. The desktop builds don't have that limitation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Once connected, exporting to Immich works exactly like exporting to disk:
    select your photos, choose the Immich tab, and confirm. Your adjustments
    are baked in before the upload, so what lands on the server is the
    finished photo.
  </p>
</Docs>
